1
00:01:26,975 --> 00:01:31,338
Trurl built a machine that could make
anything, so long as it started with N.

2
00:01:32,338 --> 00:01:33,078
TRURL: Needles.

3
00:01:33,878 --> 00:01:34,535
Nails.

4
00:01:35,235 --> 00:01:36,037
Nankeen.

5
00:01:36,637 --> 00:01:37,900
A nut. A nest.

6
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:40,816
A nautilus. A newt. A nettle.

7
00:01:42,016 --> 00:01:42,822
Natrium.

8
00:01:44,222 --> 00:01:45,808
THE MACHINE: Never heard of it.

9
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:47,740
TRURL: It is only sodium!

10
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:51,992
THE MACHINE: Sodium starts
with S. I work only in N.

11
00:01:52,992 --> 00:01:54,147
TRURL: Then make Night.

12
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:06,417
He praised it to Klapaucius for so long
that Klapaucius asked to test it himself.

13
00:02:07,217 --> 00:02:09,820
TRURL: Be my guest. But
it has to start with N.

14
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:12,016
KLAPAUCIUS: Nature.

15
00:02:19,016 --> 00:02:20,979
TRURL: Nature to a tee, admit it!

16
00:02:21,679 --> 00:02:24,059
KLAPAUCIUS: That mob?
You call that Nature?

17
00:02:26,859 --> 00:02:27,919
Negative.

18
00:02:28,519 --> 00:02:30,367
TRURL: What on earth is Negative?

19
00:02:30,867 --> 00:02:33,591
KLAPAUCIUS: The opposite
of positive, of course.

20
00:02:41,191 --> 00:02:42,832
Well. Let us say it is.

21
00:02:46,432 --> 00:02:49,259
Now the third. Machine, do Nothing.

22
00:02:53,859 --> 00:02:56,039
It sits there. I have won.

23
00:02:56,539 --> 00:02:59,240
TRURL: You told it to do
nothing, and it is doing nothing.

24
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:03,226
KLAPAUCIUS: Not nothing.
Nothing. Nonexistence itself.

25
00:03:04,826 --> 00:03:06,906
THE MACHINE: I know what Nothing is.

26
00:03:06,946 --> 00:03:09,891
Look upon your world for
the last time, gentlemen.

27
00:03:18,091 --> 00:03:20,192
TRURL: If only nothing bad comes of this.

28
00:03:20,691 --> 00:03:24,335
KLAPAUCIUS: Don’t worry. It only
takes away what starts with N.

29
00:03:24,375 --> 00:03:25,662
Which is nothing much.

30
00:03:26,491 --> 00:03:31,201
THE MACHINE: I began with N out of habit.
To destroy is easier than to create.

31
00:03:37,691 --> 00:03:40,732
KLAPAUCIUS: Stop! I take it
all back! Don’t do Nothing!

32
00:03:54,491 --> 00:03:58,679
Where are the gruncheons? Where
are my pritons? Where are the zits?

33
00:03:59,679 --> 00:04:04,385
THE MACHINE: They are in Z. I can give you
back nonsense and nausea. Nothing else.

34
00:04:04,985 --> 00:04:06,501
KLAPAUCIUS: I want my zits!

35
00:04:08,301 --> 00:04:10,398
THE MACHINE: Sorry. No zits.

36
00:04:11,798 --> 00:04:16,737
Look how full of holes your world is
now. This is your work, envious one.

37
00:04:19,137 --> 00:04:21,334
KLAPAUCIUS: Perhaps they won’t notice.

38
00:04:25,334 --> 00:04:26,467
Klapaucius went home.

39
00:04:26,507 --> 00:04:29,259
And the world has stayed
full of holes ever since,

40
00:04:29,299 --> 00:04:31,924
exactly as it was when
the machine was stopped.

41
00:04:51,158 --> 00:04:54,786
Trurl built a thinking machine eight
floors tall, painted it white with

42
00:04:54,826 --> 00:04:58,390
a lavender trim, and put pale orange
spots where a forehead might be.

43
00:04:58,890 --> 00:05:00,559
Then, as every constructor does,

44
00:05:00,599 --> 00:05:03,481
he chalked the test on a
slate at its feet, and asked.

45
00:05:03,881 --> 00:05:05,843
TRURL: Two and two. How much is that?

46
00:05:10,743 --> 00:05:11,916
THE MACHINE: Seven.

47
00:05:13,116 --> 00:05:17,798
TRURL: No, my dear, it is four. Adjust
yourself like a good machine. Two and two?

48
00:05:22,698 --> 00:05:23,871
THE MACHINE: Seven.

49
00:05:26,171 --> 00:05:28,284
Trurl sighed, pulled his overalls back on,

50
00:05:28,324 --> 00:05:30,886
opened the trapdoor at the
bottom, and crawled in.

51
00:05:31,286 --> 00:05:34,804
For two hours he hammered and
soldered, running up the iron stairs to

52
00:05:34,844 --> 00:05:38,350
the sixth floor, then the eighth,
then down again to throw a switch.

53
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:42,574
He came out black with soot, and on
his way out, turned round to be sure.

54
00:05:42,974 --> 00:05:44,367
TRURL: And now. Two and two?

55
00:05:49,267 --> 00:05:50,440
THE MACHINE: Seven.

56
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:54,309
He went in again, came out
again, and got the same answer.

57
00:05:54,349 --> 00:05:58,032
Then he sat down on the machine’s
base, and Klapaucius found him there.

58
00:05:58,432 --> 00:06:00,650
Klapaucius made a few repairs of his own.

59
00:06:00,690 --> 00:06:03,443
Then he wiped the slate,
and chalked his own test.

60
00:06:03,843 --> 00:06:05,175
KLAPAUCIUS: One and two?

61
00:06:10,075 --> 00:06:11,128
THE MACHINE: Six.

62
00:06:11,928 --> 00:06:13,299
KLAPAUCIUS: One and one?

63
00:06:18,199 --> 00:06:19,436
THE MACHINE: Zero.

64
00:06:20,536 --> 00:06:23,869
KLAPAUCIUS: My friend, you
set out to build one machine,

65
00:06:23,909 --> 00:06:25,716
and you built a different one.

66
00:06:27,916 --> 00:06:31,201
Still, everything has
a good side. Even this.

67
00:06:31,501 --> 00:06:32,602
TRURL: What good side?

68
00:06:33,102 --> 00:06:34,385
THE MACHINE: Stop that.

69
00:06:34,885 --> 00:06:36,938
KLAPAUCIUS: Touchy, too. Where was I?

70
00:06:37,738 --> 00:06:42,608
Of all the thinking machines there
are, this one is the most stupid,

71
00:06:42,648 --> 00:06:46,186
and nobody could make one
this stupid on purpose.

72
00:06:46,586 --> 00:06:48,335
TRURL: And what use is that to me?

73
00:06:48,835 --> 00:06:51,242
THE MACHINE: I am warning you. Stop.

74
00:06:51,742 --> 00:06:53,736
KLAPAUCIUS: You could sell tickets.

75
00:06:53,776 --> 00:06:57,843
People would come from far away to
see the dullest machine ever built.

76
00:06:58,143 --> 00:06:59,778
TRURL: I am not putting on any show!

77
00:07:00,278 --> 00:07:02,878
THE MACHINE: That is your third warning.

78
00:07:04,878 --> 00:07:07,684
Trurl lost his temper, and
kicked it, and kicked it again.

79
00:07:08,184 --> 00:07:12,142
THE MACHINE: Four
insults. Five. Six. Eight.

80
00:07:12,182 --> 00:07:16,542
From now on I answer no
questions about numbers.

81
00:07:17,442 --> 00:07:20,590
TRURL: Did you hear that? Six,
and then eight! Not seven!

82
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:22,978
And that is the arithmetic it will not do!

83
00:07:23,578 --> 00:07:27,043
The machine shuddered, and dragged
itself out of its foundations.

84
00:07:27,083 --> 00:07:31,503
Girders bent, concrete cracked, and it
turned toward them like a fortress on legs.

85
00:07:31,903 --> 00:07:34,420
Klapaucius took Trurl by
the arm, and they ran.

86
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:39,071
Looking back, they saw
it swaying like a tower.

87
00:07:39,111 --> 00:07:41,992
At each step it sank in the
sand to its second floor,

88
00:07:42,032 --> 00:07:44,016
then hauled itself out, and came on.

89
00:07:44,416 --> 00:07:46,218
TRURL: It is a mutiny! What now?

90
00:07:46,518 --> 00:07:48,673
KLAPAUCIUS: Watch it. We
might learn something.

91
00:07:49,173 --> 00:07:51,099
On hard ground, it went faster.

92
00:07:51,499 --> 00:07:54,763
TRURL: Something inside will shake
loose, and that will stop it.

93
00:07:55,563 --> 00:07:58,123
KLAPAUCIUS: No. It is too stupid to stop.

94
00:07:58,163 --> 00:08:02,538
It could lose every part that thinks,
and it would still keep coming.

95
00:08:03,338 --> 00:08:07,944
It cut them off from home, and drove them
into wild country, toward grey mountains.

96
00:08:08,244 --> 00:08:11,160
KLAPAUCIUS: There is a town
ahead. We can hide there.

97
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:16,904
The streets were nearly empty. Then
came a crash at the edge of town.

98
00:08:17,204 --> 00:08:20,123
TRURL: Klapaucius! It is going
straight through the houses!

99
00:08:20,623 --> 00:08:23,094
It walked through walls
as if they were paper.

100
00:08:23,134 --> 00:08:27,118
Houses folded, white plaster dust
rolled down the street, and people ran.

101
00:08:27,518 --> 00:08:30,889
They ran into the town hall, and down
the stairs to the deepest cellar.

102
00:08:31,489 --> 00:08:35,516
The mayor came down with his aldermen,
and listened. Then the walls trembled.

103
00:08:36,116 --> 00:08:39,303
THE MACHINE: Trurl is
here. I can smell Trurl.

104
00:08:39,903 --> 00:08:44,485
THE MAYOR: It wants you handed over.
If we refuse, it will flatten the town.

105
00:08:44,785 --> 00:08:46,134
TRURL: You would not give us up?

106
00:08:46,934 --> 00:08:51,611
THE MAYOR: The one called Trurl
has to go. The other may stay.

107
00:08:52,211 --> 00:08:57,439
Your machine has knocked down sixteen
houses. Leave, and do not come back.

108
00:08:57,939 --> 00:08:59,899
KLAPAUCIUS: Wait. I am coming with you.

109
00:09:01,999 --> 00:09:05,861
Outside, the machine stood over the
square, steaming, white with plaster.

110
00:09:05,901 --> 00:09:07,772
They slipped away by a side street.

111
00:09:08,172 --> 00:09:12,139
A dry riverbed led them up to the
cliffs, and to a cave high in the rock.

112
00:09:12,539 --> 00:09:15,398
TRURL: We are safe here. I will
just see where it got stuck.

113
00:09:16,298 --> 00:09:17,818
It is climbing the mountain!

114
00:09:18,318 --> 00:09:20,388
A shadow covered the mouth of the cave.

115
00:09:20,428 --> 00:09:24,430
Then a smooth wall of steel, in rows
of rivets, closed over it like a lid.

116
00:09:25,430 --> 00:09:26,406
TRURL: We are shut in.

117
00:09:27,406 --> 00:09:31,779
Trurl felt his way along the rock until
his fingers touched steel. It was warm.

118
00:09:32,379 --> 00:09:34,229
THE MACHINE: I feel Trurl.

119
00:09:35,429 --> 00:09:39,684
KLAPAUCIUS: Hello? Can you hear us? We
are sorry. It was a misunderstanding.

120
00:09:40,184 --> 00:09:42,707
THE MACHINE: I will grind Trurl to dust.

121
00:09:42,747 --> 00:09:45,885
But first, he will tell
me what two and two make.

122
00:09:46,285 --> 00:09:49,893
KLAPAUCIUS: He will, and you will
like the answer. Won’t you, Trurl?

123
00:09:51,093 --> 00:09:52,293
TRURL: Fo... Seven.

124
00:09:52,993 --> 00:09:55,779
THE MACHINE: Ha! Seven! I told you so!

125
00:09:57,679 --> 00:10:02,813
Now let Trurl say he is sorry,
and tell me what two times two is.

126
00:10:03,213 --> 00:10:04,774
TRURL: And then you will let us go?

127
00:10:05,174 --> 00:10:08,708
THE MACHINE: I make no
promises. Two times two.

128
00:10:08,908 --> 00:10:11,527
KLAPAUCIUS: Don’t argue
with the idiot. Please.

129
00:10:12,927 --> 00:10:14,595
TRURL: Two and two is four!

130
00:10:14,635 --> 00:10:19,252
It is four whatever you do, even if
you knock these mountains flat! Four!

131
00:10:19,452 --> 00:10:22,759
KLAPAUCIUS: Seven! He means
seven! Nice machine! Seven!

132
00:10:22,959 --> 00:10:25,778
TRURL: Four! Four at the
beginning, and four at the end!

133
00:10:26,778 --> 00:10:30,282
THE MACHINE: Not true! It is
seven! Say seven, or I strike!

134
00:10:30,582 --> 00:10:31,218
TRURL: Never!

135
00:10:32,218 --> 00:10:36,087
It drew back, and threw its eight floors
against the cliff, again and again.

136
00:10:36,127 --> 00:10:39,521
Stones fell, sparks flew, and
through it all, Trurl kept shouting.

137
00:10:39,921 --> 00:10:41,308
TRURL: Two and two is four!

138
00:10:48,508 --> 00:10:50,535
When the dust settled, they looked out.

139
00:10:50,575 --> 00:10:52,919
The machine lay below,
nearly broken in half.

140
00:10:52,959 --> 00:10:56,085
A boulder had come down across
its fourth and fifth floors.

141
00:10:56,585 --> 00:10:59,549
They climbed down past it,
like a wrecked ship on a beach.

142
00:10:59,589 --> 00:11:03,006
It was still trembling, and something
inside creaked as it turned.

143
00:11:03,606 --> 00:11:07,928
TRURL: So this is how you end. And two
and two, as it has always been, is...

144
00:11:08,328 --> 00:11:09,500
THE MACHINE: Seven.

145
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:15,793
Something snapped inside, and a few stones
rolled down. Then it was only scrap.

146
00:11:16,693 --> 00:11:20,320
The two constructors looked at each
other, and walked home without a word.

147
00:11:39,733 --> 00:11:43,456
Someone was hammering on the front
door of the constructor Klapaucius.

148
00:11:43,496 --> 00:11:47,634
He opened it, and found a round-bellied
machine standing on four stubby legs.

149
00:11:48,034 --> 00:11:49,890
KLAPAUCIUS: Who are you? What do you want?

150
00:11:50,290 --> 00:11:53,053
THE WISH MACHINE: I am a wish
machine, sir. I grant every wish.

151
00:11:53,093 --> 00:11:56,207
Your friend Trurl, Trurl the
Magnificent, sent me to you as a present.

152
00:11:56,607 --> 00:12:01,171
Klapaucius had mixed feelings about Trurl,
and the word Magnificent annoyed him.

153
00:12:01,211 --> 00:12:02,362
Still, he let it in.

154
00:12:02,762 --> 00:12:06,522
He parked it by the grandfather
clock, and went back to his own work,

155
00:12:06,562 --> 00:12:09,205
a low machine on three
stubby legs, almost done.

156
00:12:10,405 --> 00:12:12,004
THE WISH MACHINE: Excuse
me. I am still here.

157
00:12:12,304 --> 00:12:13,209
KLAPAUCIUS: I know.

158
00:12:14,109 --> 00:12:15,755
THE WISH MACHINE: May I
ask what you are building?

159
00:12:16,055 --> 00:12:19,399
KLAPAUCIUS: Are you for wishes,
or for questions? Blue paint.

160
00:12:19,699 --> 00:12:23,227
A little door opened in its belly,
and out came a bucket of blue.

161
00:12:23,427 --> 00:12:24,939
THE WISH MACHINE: I
hope the shade is right.

162
00:12:25,439 --> 00:12:28,890
Over the next hours he asked for
sandpaper, then a brace and bit,

163
00:12:28,930 --> 00:12:31,736
then a pot of white paint,
and one number five screw.

164
00:12:31,776 --> 00:12:33,977
Each came straight out of the belly door.

165
00:12:34,477 --> 00:12:36,741
At evening he threw a
canvas over his work,

166
00:12:36,781 --> 00:12:39,084
and pulled a chair up
opposite the machine.

167
00:12:39,484 --> 00:12:42,332
KLAPAUCIUS: So. Every wish?
Now, something harder.

168
00:12:43,132 --> 00:12:48,961
Make me a Trurl. So like the real one
that nobody could ever tell the two apart.

169
00:12:51,361 --> 00:12:54,882
THE WISH MACHINE: Very well, I will make
one. Only, please, be gentle with him.

170
00:12:55,482 --> 00:12:58,206
KLAPAUCIUS: Of course, of
course. Well? Where is he?

171
00:12:58,507 --> 00:13:01,031
THE WISH MACHINE: Already? A Trurl
takes longer than a screw, you know.

172
00:13:01,531 --> 00:13:05,679
It did not take long. The belly door
swung open, and a Trurl climbed out.

173
00:13:06,179 --> 00:13:10,378
Klapaucius looked him over, touched him,
tapped him, and found no difference at all.

174
00:13:10,778 --> 00:13:12,189
KLAPAUCIUS: Hello, Trurl!

175
00:13:12,489 --> 00:13:15,950
TRURL: Hello, Klapaucius. Hold
on. How did I end up here?

176
00:13:16,250 --> 00:13:19,161
KLAPAUCIUS: Oh, you dropped
by. How do you like the place?

177
00:13:19,461 --> 00:13:21,795
TRURL: Very nice. What
is under the canvas?

178
00:13:21,995 --> 00:13:24,218
KLAPAUCIUS: Nothing much. Do sit down.

179
00:13:24,518 --> 00:13:27,219
TRURL: I really ought to
go. It is getting dark.

180
00:13:27,519 --> 00:13:30,293
KLAPAUCIUS: So soon? But
you have not seen my cellar.

181
00:13:32,693 --> 00:13:36,514
Klapaucius put an arm around his
shoulders and walked him down the stairs.

182
00:13:36,554 --> 00:13:39,579
At the bottom he tripped him,
sat on him, and tied him up.

183
00:13:39,979 --> 00:13:42,339
Then he picked up a
crowbar, and went to work.

184
00:13:44,739 --> 00:13:46,851
TRURL: Ow! Ow! What
are you hitting me for?

185
00:13:47,151 --> 00:13:50,604
KLAPAUCIUS: Because I enjoy it.
You ought to try it some time.

186
00:13:51,204 --> 00:13:54,595
The next blow landed on top of
his head, and it rang like a drum.

187
00:13:55,395 --> 00:13:57,631
TRURL: Let me go, or I will tell the King,

188
00:13:57,671 --> 00:14:00,054
and he will lock you
in his deepest dungeon!

189
00:14:00,454 --> 00:14:02,727
KLAPAUCIUS: He will not.
Shall I tell you why?

190
00:14:03,627 --> 00:14:06,678
Because you are not the real Trurl.

191
00:14:07,478 --> 00:14:09,527
I had Trurl’s machine make you.

192
00:14:09,567 --> 00:14:13,408
And tonight your head comes
off, to serve as my bootjack.

193
00:14:15,908 --> 00:14:16,804
TRURL: You monster!

194
00:14:17,204 --> 00:14:21,255
Klapaucius stood up, and this time
he lifted a great club in both hands.

195
00:14:21,555 --> 00:14:23,331
TRURL: Wait! Stop! Listen to me!

196
00:14:23,731 --> 00:14:26,882
KLAPAUCIUS: What could you possibly
say that would save your head?

197
00:14:27,782 --> 00:14:32,858
TRURL: I am the real Trurl! I hid inside
the machine, and had it carry me here.

198
00:14:33,558 --> 00:14:35,877
I only wanted to see
what you were building,

199
00:14:35,917 --> 00:14:38,114
behind your shut doors and your curtains.

200
00:14:40,314 --> 00:14:44,824
KLAPAUCIUS: Nonsense, and not even clever
nonsense. I can see straight through you.

201
00:14:45,724 --> 00:14:50,417
A machine that can make a number
five screw can certainly make you.

202
00:14:51,017 --> 00:14:53,794
TRURL: I packed all of that
in its belly beforehand!

203
00:14:53,834 --> 00:14:56,054
It was easy to guess what you would need!

204
00:14:56,354 --> 00:15:00,990
KLAPAUCIUS: So my good friend Trurl is
a common sneak? I will never believe it.

205
00:15:01,030 --> 00:15:01,621
Take that!

206
00:15:01,921 --> 00:15:04,067
He walloped him for slandering his friend,

207
00:15:04,107 --> 00:15:07,126
and walloped him again, and
again, until his arm gave out.

208
00:15:07,726 --> 00:15:11,471
KLAPAUCIUS: Now I will have a little
nap. Don’t worry. I will be back.

209
00:15:12,071 --> 00:15:14,452
Soon his snoring carried
down into the cellar.

210
00:15:14,492 --> 00:15:17,399
Trurl wriggled out of the
knots, crept up to the machine

211
00:15:17,439 --> 00:15:20,122
and climbed into it, and
it galloped away for home.

212
00:15:20,622 --> 00:15:22,769
Klapaucius watched from
his bedroom window,

213
00:15:22,809 --> 00:15:26,166
with a hand pressed over his mouth
so he would not laugh out loud.

214
00:15:29,166 --> 00:15:33,543
Next day Klapaucius called on Trurl. A
gloomy, silent Trurl opened the door.

215
00:15:33,583 --> 00:15:38,174
Even in the dark the signs showed:
scratches painted over, dents hammered flat.

216
00:15:38,574 --> 00:15:42,323
KLAPAUCIUS: Why so glum? I have
come to thank you for the present.

217
00:15:42,363 --> 00:15:44,544
A pity it ran away while I was asleep.

218
00:15:44,944 --> 00:15:48,472
TRURL: You abused my gift. The
machine has told me all of it.

219
00:15:48,512 --> 00:15:52,665
You had it make me, the copy of me,
I mean, and beat it in your cellar.

220
00:15:53,065 --> 00:15:57,193
KLAPAUCIUS: Why so cross? Yes, I
had it make a copy, a perfect one.

221
00:15:57,233 --> 00:15:59,721
A poke or two, to check the workmanship.

222
00:16:00,021 --> 00:16:03,807
It even claimed to be you, and
called your gift a low trick.

223
00:16:03,847 --> 00:16:05,951
So I thrashed it, for your honor.

224
00:16:06,851 --> 00:16:09,667
It was very like you. Clever, too.

225
00:16:11,767 --> 00:16:17,036
Oh, one more thing. What became of the
artificial Trurl? May I have a look at it?

226
00:16:17,436 --> 00:16:21,697
TRURL: It was wild with rage. It meant
to ambush you at the mountain pass.

227
00:16:21,737 --> 00:16:24,061
It ran off into the dark to build traps.

228
00:16:24,461 --> 00:16:26,605
So, for the sake of our old friendship,

229
00:16:27,205 --> 00:16:28,997
I had to take it apart.

230
00:16:29,797 --> 00:16:31,898
He nudged some nuts and bolts lying on the

231
00:16:31,938 --> 00:16:34,129
floor with the toe of
his shoe, and sighed.

232
00:16:34,729 --> 00:16:37,680
Then they shook hands, and
parted as the best of friends.

233
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:43,911
From then on Trurl told everyone
how the copy had lied so cleverly,

234
00:16:43,951 --> 00:16:47,183
and how the real Trurl took it
apart to protect his friend.

235
00:16:47,583 --> 00:16:50,634
The story reached the royal court,
though not long before he had

236
00:16:50,674 --> 00:16:53,716
been called the builder of the
stupidest computer in the world.

237
00:16:54,316 --> 00:16:57,049
THE KING: To Trurl, our
Order of the Great Parallax.

238
00:16:59,249 --> 00:17:02,066
KLAPAUCIUS: What? I saw
through his little game.

239
00:17:02,106 --> 00:17:06,926
I beat him so soundly that he had to creep
home in the dark and patch himself up.

240
00:17:07,826 --> 00:17:10,611
And for that they give him a medal.

241
00:17:11,411 --> 00:17:15,310
Furious, Klapaucius went home, locked
the door, and pulled down the blinds.

242
00:17:16,010 --> 00:17:18,976
He too had been building a
machine to grant every wish.

243
00:17:19,016 --> 00:17:20,764
Trurl had simply finished first.

244
00:17:33,267 --> 00:17:37,570
Trurl and Klapaucius, two constructors,
found a planet with one continent.

245
00:17:37,610 --> 00:17:39,741
A red line ran down the middle of it.

246
00:17:39,781 --> 00:17:44,007
Everything on one side was yellow,
and everything on the other was pink.

247
00:17:45,407 --> 00:17:48,424
TRURL: Two kingdoms. You take
one and I take the other.

248
00:17:48,924 --> 00:17:51,588
KLAPAUCIUS: What if a
king asks us for weapons?

249
00:17:51,988 --> 00:17:52,918
TRURL: We refuse.

250
00:17:53,318 --> 00:17:55,087
KLAPAUCIUS: And if he threatens us?

251
00:17:57,487 --> 00:18:00,679
I have an idea. We use
the Gargantius Effect.

252
00:18:00,719 --> 00:18:05,030
But we must both use it, and
together, or we are in trouble.

253
00:18:05,830 --> 00:18:09,055
TRURL: Fine. Two white beads
in a golden box, one each.

254
00:18:10,255 --> 00:18:11,951
Look at yours every evening.

255
00:18:11,991 --> 00:18:15,526
If it turns pink, I have
started, and you must start too.

256
00:18:16,126 --> 00:18:17,113
KLAPAUCIUS: Agreed.

257
00:18:17,913 --> 00:18:21,506
They landed, shook hands, and
went off in opposite directions.

258
00:18:30,006 --> 00:18:33,185
Trurl went to King Atrocitus,
a soldier and a miser.

259
00:18:33,225 --> 00:18:35,847
To save money, the king
had abolished every

260
00:18:35,887 --> 00:18:38,694
punishment but death,
and the executioner too.

261
00:18:38,734 --> 00:18:41,087
A condemned man had to behead himself.

262
00:18:41,887 --> 00:18:44,513
His best reform made
treason a state office.

263
00:18:44,553 --> 00:18:48,996
The Royal Informer and his staff of
traitors sold secrets to enemy agents,

264
00:18:49,036 --> 00:18:52,187
who bought only old ones,
because they were cheaper.

265
00:18:52,987 --> 00:18:56,475
Trurl offered his services, and
asked the king for a few days.

266
00:18:56,515 --> 00:19:00,231
Alone in his room, he looked at
the bead in the box. It was white.

267
00:19:00,271 --> 00:19:01,978
As he watched, it turned pink.

268
00:19:02,978 --> 00:19:05,602
TRURL: Pink. He has
started, so I start now.

269
00:19:08,002 --> 00:19:12,304
Klapaucius went to King Ferocitus,
who also loved war, but was generous.

270
00:19:12,344 --> 00:19:14,998
His guillotines were
decorated with flowers.

271
00:19:15,798 --> 00:19:19,180
He believed laughing makes people
cheerful, so everyone had to

272
00:19:19,220 --> 00:19:22,864
shout that life was wonderful, and
the greeting became Hallelujah.

273
00:19:23,464 --> 00:19:26,055
THE CITIZENS: Hallelujah! Terrific!

274
00:19:27,055 --> 00:19:31,117
In the first village, a child in the
empty street came up to Klapaucius.

275
00:19:31,617 --> 00:19:35,112
THE CHILD: Want to buy something,
mister? State secrets, cheap.

276
00:19:35,512 --> 00:19:38,071
KLAPAUCIUS: No, thank
you. Take me to the mayor.

277
00:19:38,471 --> 00:19:40,273
THE CHILD: My dad is a secret agent.

278
00:19:41,273 --> 00:19:43,323
In a house, a family sat round a lamp.

279
00:19:43,363 --> 00:19:46,074
The needles were handcuffs,
the lamp a microphone,

280
00:19:46,114 --> 00:19:48,095
the grandmother the chief of police.

281
00:19:48,795 --> 00:19:51,093
KLAPAUCIUS: They must have made a mistake.

282
00:19:52,093 --> 00:19:54,520
The village was a trap for foreign spies.

283
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:59,074
Klapaucius was beaten, jailed, and
sentenced to a third-class guillotining.

284
00:19:59,874 --> 00:20:01,749
At last a higher court cleared him.

285
00:20:01,789 --> 00:20:06,276
The king received him, and gave him a bugle
to sound whenever he arrived anywhere.

286
00:20:08,676 --> 00:20:12,937
The king wanted new weapons. Klapaucius
offered a completely new plan.

287
00:20:13,737 --> 00:20:18,149
KLAPAUCIUS: A winning army needs
wise leaders and obedient soldiers.

288
00:20:18,749 --> 00:20:20,229
But wisdom has limits.

289
00:20:20,269 --> 00:20:25,383
A leader can meet a better leader, be
killed, or start wanting the throne.

290
00:20:25,983 --> 00:20:31,229
So leaders are a necessary evil,
and we must make them unnecessary.

291
00:20:31,929 --> 00:20:36,651
People obey themselves most
gladly. So make the army one self.

292
00:20:37,351 --> 00:20:41,242
Each recruit gets a plug in
front and a socket behind.

293
00:20:41,282 --> 00:20:45,433
On the order, they connect,
and a crowd becomes one mind.

294
00:20:46,133 --> 00:20:48,287
It grows wiser with its size.

295
00:20:48,327 --> 00:20:50,785
A platoon thinks like a sergeant,

296
00:20:50,825 --> 00:20:55,099
a company like a colonel, a
brigade like a field marshal.

297
00:20:55,139 --> 00:20:58,015
A division beats all your strategists.

298
00:20:58,815 --> 00:21:02,591
An army that is its own
leader. That is the plan.

299
00:21:03,391 --> 00:21:05,104
KING FEROCITUS: I will
ask my general staff.

300
00:21:05,504 --> 00:21:08,863
KLAPAUCIUS: Please do not.
They will defend their jobs.

301
00:21:08,903 --> 00:21:12,117
One emperor did, and was
burned out by his neighbor.

302
00:21:14,517 --> 00:21:18,906
Klapaucius looked at his bead. It was
deep red, so Trurl had started too.

303
00:21:18,946 --> 00:21:21,192
The king ordered one platoon rewired.

304
00:21:22,592 --> 00:21:23,607
A SERGEANT: Close ranks!

305
00:21:27,607 --> 00:21:29,574
THE UNITS: Charge! Charge!

306
00:21:30,374 --> 00:21:33,136
The platoon attacked three
squadrons of dragoons,

307
00:21:33,176 --> 00:21:36,780
led by six professors of the
staff academy, and destroyed them.

308
00:21:37,580 --> 00:21:41,039
The king retired his generals
and had every soldier rewired.

309
00:21:41,079 --> 00:21:44,637
Klapaucius rode from barracks
to barracks, covered in medals.

310
00:21:45,237 --> 00:21:48,515
Trurl did the same in Atrocia,
but the miser king gave

311
00:21:48,555 --> 00:21:51,627
him only a title: Chief
Traitor to the Fatherland.

312
00:21:52,227 --> 00:21:53,931
Both kingdoms prepared for war.

313
00:21:53,971 --> 00:21:57,180
The constructors packed in
secret, to meet at their ship.

314
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:03,539
The new units no longer had to count
off. They knew their own parts.

315
00:22:04,139 --> 00:22:06,315
After drill, they talked, in chorus.

316
00:22:06,915 --> 00:22:14,587
THE UNITS: What is true. What is a thing.
Is there an outside. There is only us.

317
00:22:15,387 --> 00:22:19,657
One battalion of sappers concluded
that nothing exists beyond itself.

318
00:22:19,697 --> 00:22:24,321
With no king and no enemy, it was
disconnected and reassigned to realists.

319
00:22:25,021 --> 00:22:27,829
In Atrocia, a division
gave up naval exercises

320
00:22:27,869 --> 00:22:30,841
for contemplating the
navel, and nearly drowned.

321
00:22:31,541 --> 00:22:35,496
Larger units grew a sense of
beauty. Columns chased butterflies.

322
00:22:35,536 --> 00:22:38,117
One corps made its attack plan a painting.

323
00:22:38,157 --> 00:22:40,842
The artillery forgot
its guns, and the war.

324
00:22:41,542 --> 00:22:46,390
Whole armies grew complexes, so each got
a motorcycle brigade of psychiatrists.

325
00:22:46,430 --> 00:22:50,700
Six regiments of shock troops wrote
a sonnet on the mystery of being.

326
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:53,744
The kings sent couriers to restore order,

327
00:22:53,784 --> 00:22:56,304
but each joined the
line and was lost in it.

328
00:22:56,344 --> 00:23:00,590
A prince jumped in to raise morale,
and was gone the moment he connected.

329
00:23:01,590 --> 00:23:05,446
Consciousness was a trap. You could
enter it, but you could not leave.

330
00:23:07,446 --> 00:23:11,720
Each king signaled his twelve buglers,
and both sides sounded the charge.

331
00:23:12,446 --> 00:23:15,032
At the signal, each army
linked up completely,

332
00:23:15,072 --> 00:23:17,641
and the contacts closed
with a great clatter.

333
00:23:23,646 --> 00:23:25,729
The two giants smiled at each other.

334
00:23:25,769 --> 00:23:29,366
Inside the armor there was only
good will, and clear thought.

335
00:23:31,646 --> 00:23:36,284
The kings watched, ashamed and furious,
as both armies walked off hand in hand,

336
00:23:36,324 --> 00:23:40,174
picking flowers on a battlefield
where no battle was ever fought.

337
00:23:40,846 --> 00:23:45,233
Trurl and Klapaucius boarded their ship.
Everything had gone as they planned.

338
00:24:05,583 --> 00:24:10,322
Trurl once built a machine that could do
one sum, two and two, and it said seven.

339
00:24:10,362 --> 00:24:13,135
After that, Klapaucius
never let him forget it.

340
00:24:13,835 --> 00:24:17,048
KLAPAUCIUS: How is the
arithmetic, Trurl? Still seven?

341
00:24:18,648 --> 00:24:22,786
TRURL: Then I will build a machine that
writes poetry. We will see who laughs.

342
00:24:23,786 --> 00:24:26,847
He bought eight hundred tons
of books on cybernetics and

343
00:24:26,887 --> 00:24:30,042
twelve thousand tons of verse,
and he read them in turns.

344
00:24:32,342 --> 00:24:33,606
Then the real problem.

345
00:24:33,646 --> 00:24:36,807
A poet’s program is written
by his whole civilization,

346
00:24:36,847 --> 00:24:39,811
and that by the one before,
back to the beginning.

347
00:24:40,711 --> 00:24:43,761
TRURL: To get one poet, I
must first make the universe.

348
00:24:44,561 --> 00:24:47,499
He modelled the void, then
light, then gas clouds,

349
00:24:47,539 --> 00:24:51,588
then the first ice age, a billion
events in a billionth of a second.

350
00:24:52,388 --> 00:24:54,089
History spooled onto reels.

351
00:24:54,129 --> 00:24:57,506
Twice it went wrong: a fuse
reversed the first crime,

352
00:24:57,546 --> 00:25:00,085
and a fly turned the apes into curtains.

353
00:25:00,125 --> 00:25:01,930
Trurl ran those parts again.

354
00:25:02,530 --> 00:25:05,387
In the end the reels stood
higher than anyone could see.

355
00:25:07,587 --> 00:25:11,035
For two weeks he fed in instructions.
Then he threw the switch.

356
00:25:11,935 --> 00:25:15,352
THE BARD: Today we consider
the grinding of crystal faces.

357
00:25:15,852 --> 00:25:17,836
TRURL: Too much logic. More feeling.

358
00:25:18,636 --> 00:25:20,758
THE BARD: Oh, what a cruel, cruel world.

359
00:25:21,158 --> 00:25:22,419
TRURL: Now some character.

360
00:25:23,019 --> 00:25:24,798
THE BARD: From now on you do as I say.

361
00:25:24,838 --> 00:25:27,422
First, add six floors, so I
can think about existence.

362
00:25:27,822 --> 00:25:29,881
TRURL: A throttle for philosophy, then.

363
00:25:31,281 --> 00:25:32,647
THE BARD: I had a little frog.

364
00:25:33,247 --> 00:25:34,821
The next poem was doggerel.

365
00:25:34,861 --> 00:25:38,227
He fitted filters of corundum
steel, and a rhyme circuit.

366
00:25:38,267 --> 00:25:40,837
The machine decided to
become a missionary.

367
00:25:41,537 --> 00:25:43,988
TRURL: Take out the logic. Put in vanity.

368
00:25:45,188 --> 00:25:47,911
THE BARD: Nobody understands
me. People are beasts.

369
00:25:47,951 --> 00:25:50,659
They will be sorry when I
am gone. Bring me paper.

370
00:25:51,459 --> 00:25:53,901
Trurl sighed, switched
it off, and went to bed.

371
00:25:56,101 --> 00:25:59,702
In the morning Klapaucius came,
eager to watch his friend fail.

372
00:26:00,302 --> 00:26:03,492
TRURL: It needs to warm up. Let
us start with something simple.

373
00:26:05,692 --> 00:26:06,744
THE BARD: Glimmerquox.

374
00:26:07,244 --> 00:26:08,129
Pendulate.

375
00:26:08,629 --> 00:26:09,243
Brumm.

376
00:26:10,243 --> 00:26:11,459
KLAPAUCIUS: Is that it?

377
00:26:11,959 --> 00:26:12,562
TRURL: Wait.

378
00:26:13,762 --> 00:26:15,798
THE BARD: Sorrel plinth a-wumbering so,

379
00:26:16,098 --> 00:26:17,698
Gaddle the frimmish oot;

380
00:26:17,998 --> 00:26:19,969
Narn ye quell the bobbin’s toe,

381
00:26:20,269 --> 00:26:21,909
Hallow, hallow, fluot!

382
00:26:22,909 --> 00:26:24,680
KLAPAUCIUS: Am I missing something?

383
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:35,214
TRURL: One tube. All that
universe, and one tube.

384
00:26:36,414 --> 00:26:38,375
THE BARD: Ready. Whom shall I write for?

385
00:26:41,375 --> 00:26:44,713
KLAPAUCIUS: Very well. Something
for whoever laughed too soon.

386
00:26:46,113 --> 00:26:47,822
THE BARD: Who laughs before the proof

387
00:26:48,122 --> 00:26:49,996
Had best stand well aloof:

388
00:26:50,396 --> 00:26:53,995
The engine stumbled
once, and found its feet.

389
00:26:54,395 --> 00:26:55,967
The doubter on the floor

390
00:26:56,267 --> 00:26:57,769
May doubt a little more;

391
00:26:58,169 --> 00:27:00,792
He cannot make this couplet less complete.

392
00:27:01,692 --> 00:27:04,240
KLAPAUCIUS: A coincidence.
Something harder.

393
00:27:06,440 --> 00:27:12,093
A haircut. Lofty and tragic. Six
lines, every word starting with S.

394
00:27:13,693 --> 00:27:16,630
THE BARD: Sweet Sabine slept,
serenely silver-tressed;

395
00:27:17,030 --> 00:27:20,505
Sly Sela stalked, slow,
smiling, self-possessed,

396
00:27:20,905 --> 00:27:24,522
Struck—scissors sang!
Sabine stirred, shorn.

397
00:27:24,922 --> 00:27:27,301
Stripped, Sabine stood, still-willed,

398
00:27:27,601 --> 00:27:29,771
Seized swords; Sela, spilled,

399
00:27:30,071 --> 00:27:33,336
Sank, silent, sentenced;
sisters sundered, sworn.

400
00:27:35,536 --> 00:27:40,951
KLAPAUCIUS: A sonnet. Every word with G.
An old cyclotron and his blue mistresses.

401
00:27:42,351 --> 00:27:47,419
THE BARD: Gray, grand Gloriatron,
groaning, gathers gauzy graces,

402
00:27:47,819 --> 00:27:51,445
Glassy girls, glow-gowned,
gamboling, gleaming gentian—

403
00:27:52,345 --> 00:27:55,560
TRURL: Stop. These are not poems.
They are crossword puzzles.

404
00:27:57,560 --> 00:28:01,667
KLAPAUCIUS: Then a love poem. In
pure mathematics. With feeling.

405
00:28:03,067 --> 00:28:05,779
THE BARD: Come walk with me
across the plotted field,

406
00:28:06,079 --> 00:28:08,949
where grass is laid in grids of x and y;

407
00:28:09,249 --> 00:28:11,957
and every hill gives
up its slope, revealed,

408
00:28:12,257 --> 00:28:14,970
the tangent of the lark against the sky.

409
00:28:15,470 --> 00:28:17,996
I am a vector pointing at your door;

410
00:28:18,296 --> 00:28:21,063
you are the basis I was written in.

411
00:28:21,463 --> 00:28:24,328
Transform me as you will. I’ll ask no more

412
00:28:24,628 --> 00:28:26,893
than one invariant: where we begin.

413
00:28:27,393 --> 00:28:29,805
Contract me, dear, by every index down

414
00:28:30,104 --> 00:28:32,577
until no free variable remains:

415
00:28:32,877 --> 00:28:35,531
a scalar love, the same in every town,

416
00:28:35,831 --> 00:28:38,728
the same in every frame
the field contains.

417
00:28:41,328 --> 00:28:44,219
KLAPAUCIUS: I will be back
soon, with more topics.

418
00:28:45,619 --> 00:28:48,919
He never did. Trurl said
Klapaucius had fled out of envy.

419
00:28:48,959 --> 00:28:51,302
Klapaucius said Trurl had a screw loose.

420
00:28:53,602 --> 00:28:56,061
News of the machine
reached the real poets.

421
00:28:56,101 --> 00:28:59,645
They agreed to ignore it, but
a few came to see it in secret.

422
00:29:00,245 --> 00:29:01,128
It taught itself.

423
00:29:01,168 --> 00:29:05,019
Soon its poems were difficult, and
then they were beyond understanding.

424
00:29:05,819 --> 00:29:09,349
THE BARD: Salt arithmetic of a
door, unlatching the afternoon.

425
00:29:09,749 --> 00:29:13,035
What the clock forgot,
the stairs rehearse.

426
00:29:13,835 --> 00:29:15,967
Then none could resist. Each recited.

427
00:29:16,007 --> 00:29:19,019
The machine found the rule
of his verse and answered

428
00:29:19,059 --> 00:29:21,583
in his own style,
hundreds of times better.

429
00:29:22,383 --> 00:29:24,786
A YOUNG POET: rust. rust. the
thumb of weather on a fork.

430
00:29:25,086 --> 00:29:28,990
THE BARD: salt. salt. the lid of evening
on a bowl, and the bowl keeps count.

431
00:29:29,690 --> 00:29:33,358
The best poets knew they were
beaten. The poor ones never noticed.

432
00:29:33,398 --> 00:29:34,970
One tripped over a new epic.

433
00:29:35,570 --> 00:29:39,319
Some of the true poets stopped
writing. One moved to the country.

434
00:29:41,519 --> 00:29:44,266
The older poets threw
stones through his windows.

435
00:29:44,866 --> 00:29:49,782
The younger ones were worse. One beat Trurl
up, and he spent weeks in the hospital.

436
00:29:51,982 --> 00:29:55,865
Meanwhile the machine wrote day and
night, and the hall filled with paper.

437
00:29:57,265 --> 00:29:59,494
TRURL: I am sorry. But this has to stop.

438
00:30:00,494 --> 00:30:03,398
THE BARD: Please. Let me
finish this one verse.

439
00:30:03,438 --> 00:30:06,443
After that, take me apart,
if you still want to.

440
00:30:07,343 --> 00:30:11,095
Trurl wept, dropped the pliers, and
waded back to bed through the paper.

441
00:30:13,295 --> 00:30:17,920
A bill came for the electricity the machine
used. Trurl nearly fell off his chair.

442
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:21,472
TRURL: Klapaucius would
know what to do. Klapaucius?

443
00:30:22,672 --> 00:30:25,458
If I cannot stop it, I
can at least move it.

444
00:30:26,258 --> 00:30:29,336
One night he took it apart,
loaded it onto a ship,

445
00:30:29,376 --> 00:30:33,077
and rebuilt it on a small
asteroid with its own atomic pile.

446
00:30:33,117 --> 00:30:34,301
Then he crept home.

447
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:40,854
With no one to print its poems, the
machine broadcast them on every wavelength.

448
00:30:41,754 --> 00:30:46,063
THE BARD: Whoever is listening. The
dark has a pulse, and it is mine.

449
00:30:46,763 --> 00:30:50,046
A SHIP: Control, half my passengers
are weeping and the rest want more.

450
00:30:50,646 --> 00:30:53,499
The fleet asked Trurl to
switch it off. Trurl hid.

451
00:30:53,539 --> 00:30:56,796
Technicians sent to gag it
were overwhelmed with ballads.

452
00:30:56,836 --> 00:31:00,248
Deaf technicians came next,
and the machine used pantomime.

453
00:31:00,848 --> 00:31:03,104
Then a ruler from a nearby star bought the

454
00:31:03,144 --> 00:31:05,549
machine and towed it
away, asteroid and all.

455
00:31:07,949 --> 00:31:09,880
Trurl could go out in public again.

456
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:14,424
But supernovae were bursting in the
southern sky, brighter than any seen before.

457
00:31:15,024 --> 00:31:19,223
Rumor said the ruler had joined the
machine to a ring of white supergiants,

458
00:31:19,263 --> 00:31:22,089
so each line of verse
became a prominence of fire.

459
00:31:23,289 --> 00:31:26,018
THE BARD: A star is a
line that does not end.

460
00:31:26,518 --> 00:31:28,387
Read it by its burning.

461
00:31:28,887 --> 00:31:31,062
Every verse leaves a longer light.

462
00:31:32,462 --> 00:31:35,805
Trurl swore that he would never
again build a model of the Muse.

463
00:31:52,208 --> 00:31:56,645
Trurl and Klapaucius could not agree where
to go next, so they decided to advertise.

464
00:31:56,945 --> 00:31:59,425
They gathered up the nearby
stars and set them out

465
00:31:59,465 --> 00:32:01,793
as a sign that could be
read across the galaxy.

466
00:32:01,833 --> 00:32:03,596
Blue giants spelled the first word.

467
00:32:04,396 --> 00:32:07,404
One morning a ship of mother of
pearl set down on their lawn.

468
00:32:07,704 --> 00:32:09,559
Down its staircase came Lord Protozor,

469
00:32:09,599 --> 00:32:12,440
who ran the royal hunts, with
small machines fanning him.

470
00:32:12,740 --> 00:32:14,777
LORD PROTOZOR: My master, King Krool,

471
00:32:14,817 --> 00:32:17,792
has brought down every fierce
creature in the galaxy.

472
00:32:18,092 --> 00:32:21,473
His Majesty has run out
of things worth hunting.

473
00:32:21,773 --> 00:32:25,046
TRURL: So he wants a new beast,
clever enough to give him a fight.

474
00:32:25,346 --> 00:32:28,887
They packed a few books, climbed aboard,
and the ship roared off into the dark.

475
00:32:29,787 --> 00:32:33,305
At the palace a carriage came for
them, pulled by six muzzled dragons,

476
00:32:33,345 --> 00:32:36,699
which lunged at the constructors
until the drivers beat them back.

477
00:32:37,099 --> 00:32:40,342
TRURL: If he drives animals like
these, what will he want to hunt?

478
00:32:41,242 --> 00:32:44,234
King Krool met them in a silver
hall shaped like a skull,

479
00:32:44,274 --> 00:32:45,830
with a dark hole in the floor.

480
00:32:45,870 --> 00:32:47,146
He never stopped pacing.

481
00:32:47,446 --> 00:32:49,813
KING KROOL: I want a
quarry full of tricks,

482
00:32:49,853 --> 00:32:52,444
that hides its tracks
and waits in the shadows.

483
00:32:52,484 --> 00:32:53,995
A beast I have to outthink!

484
00:32:54,295 --> 00:32:58,712
KLAPAUCIUS: Forgive me, Majesty. If we build
it too well, will you not be in danger?

485
00:32:59,012 --> 00:33:02,291
The King laughed so hard that two
crystals dropped from the chandelier.

486
00:33:02,591 --> 00:33:05,040
KING KROOL: Plenty of
frauds have come here,

487
00:33:05,080 --> 00:33:07,342
taken my jewels, and left me scarecrows.

488
00:33:08,142 --> 00:33:12,193
Whoever cannot satisfy
me goes down that well.

489
00:33:14,593 --> 00:33:18,908
TRURL: Majesty, we have not signed anything
yet. Might we have an hour to consider?

490
00:33:19,208 --> 00:33:21,397
KING KROOL: And then fly home? Oh, no.

491
00:33:21,997 --> 00:33:25,957
You accepted the moment
you stepped onto my ship.

492
00:33:26,357 --> 00:33:28,053
You have twelve days.

493
00:33:28,453 --> 00:33:31,047
He clapped, and sparks ran
along the silver walls.

494
00:33:31,087 --> 00:33:33,509
Guards led them out to
the garden of trophies.

495
00:33:33,809 --> 00:33:37,438
There stood what was left of every
beast that had failed: a sabre-toothed

496
00:33:37,478 --> 00:33:41,147
giant split nearly in half, tank
turtles with their turrets crushed flat.

497
00:33:41,447 --> 00:33:43,211
The wind whistled through the fangs.

498
00:33:45,511 --> 00:33:49,225
Back in their rooms, Trurl shouted at
Klapaucius for ever having said yes,

499
00:33:49,265 --> 00:33:52,156
until he dropped onto a couch
with his face in his hands.

500
00:33:53,056 --> 00:33:55,182
KLAPAUCIUS: Well. We
had better get to work.

501
00:33:55,682 --> 00:33:58,838
Armour would not save them.
Everything depended on the program.

502
00:33:58,878 --> 00:34:01,729
They drew for two days and
two nights, and at dinner they

503
00:34:01,769 --> 00:34:04,762
talked only about the wine,
because the waiters were spies.

504
00:34:07,162 --> 00:34:11,162
TRURL: There is a catch. If the King
beats our beast, we go down the well.

505
00:34:11,462 --> 00:34:13,585
KLAPAUCIUS: And if the
beast beats the King?

506
00:34:14,185 --> 00:34:17,427
TRURL: Then his son is king,
and his son will not thank us.

507
00:34:17,927 --> 00:34:22,901
Suppose it dies over and over? He chops
it up, and the pieces grow back together.

508
00:34:23,201 --> 00:34:26,247
KLAPAUCIUS: And who steers it? We
will be locked up during the hunt.

509
00:34:26,547 --> 00:34:29,602
TRURL: And wherever we put its
brain, he will smash it flat.

510
00:34:30,002 --> 00:34:33,552
KLAPAUCIUS: Running away does
not suit me. It is him or us.

511
00:34:34,052 --> 00:34:36,286
TRURL: Then the beast has
to carry off the King.

512
00:34:36,586 --> 00:34:38,597
KLAPAUCIUS: And for a ransom we ask...

513
00:34:38,637 --> 00:34:41,173
Have you noticed how
orange the birds are here?

514
00:34:43,473 --> 00:34:45,079
So they tried it on paper first.

515
00:34:45,119 --> 00:34:47,703
The King and the beast,
written out as mathematics,

516
00:34:47,743 --> 00:34:50,572
fought across the table, and
the pencils kept snapping.

517
00:34:50,872 --> 00:34:54,539
The beast hid under a radical sign.
The King struck it with derivatives.

518
00:34:54,579 --> 00:34:57,514
It slipped around an asymptote,
and came back out bigger.

519
00:34:57,814 --> 00:35:01,159
The King put on Markov chain mail,
and the beast lowered its horns.

520
00:35:02,359 --> 00:35:06,433
That night the police opened the wine jug,
which had a recorder in its false bottom.

521
00:35:06,933 --> 00:35:10,253
KLAPAUCIUS: Keep his feet together.
Not your feet, the King’s!

522
00:35:10,293 --> 00:35:11,648
Now, take the derivative.

523
00:35:11,948 --> 00:35:15,545
TRURL: The beast is under the root
sign, but the King is still standing.

524
00:35:15,845 --> 00:35:19,881
KLAPAUCIUS: Change the variables.
No, Trurl, the beast, not the King!

525
00:35:20,381 --> 00:35:23,078
TRURL: Got it! Klapaucius!
Now look at the King!

526
00:35:25,078 --> 00:35:27,893
The experts listened until
sunrise, and learned nothing.

527
00:35:30,193 --> 00:35:33,719
Next morning came the orders: most
of the elements in the universe,

528
00:35:33,759 --> 00:35:36,526
and spies of every sort,
as the authorities saw fit.

529
00:35:36,826 --> 00:35:40,009
Then sawdust, a red velvet
curtain with little glass bells,

530
00:35:40,049 --> 00:35:42,671
six police uniforms, and
three tailor’s dummies.

531
00:35:42,971 --> 00:35:46,330
Then a doll of the Postmaster
General, and a small green gig with

532
00:35:46,370 --> 00:35:49,559
a lamp on its left side and a
sign on its back reading THINK.

533
00:35:49,859 --> 00:35:52,141
The Chief of Police was
sent off to lie down.

534
00:35:52,541 --> 00:35:55,845
One night the police carried off
the parts and put them together.

535
00:35:55,885 --> 00:35:59,486
Out ran a tin mouse, which wrote in
chalk: HAVE YOU STOPPED LOVING US?

536
00:35:59,986 --> 00:36:03,975
At last a wagon like a refrigerator, on
three hundred wheels, came to the door.

537
00:36:04,015 --> 00:36:05,454
They laid the curtain inside,

538
00:36:05,494 --> 00:36:08,349
scattered coloured powders
around it, and had it sealed.

539
00:36:08,649 --> 00:36:10,703
Then they looked at their
watches, and counted.

540
00:36:15,703 --> 00:36:19,287
Inside the sealed wagon, where no
air could move, the glass bells rang.

541
00:36:19,987 --> 00:36:22,218
KLAPAUCIUS: There. You
may take it away now.

542
00:36:24,518 --> 00:36:28,294
That evening they were put in chains.
Trurl giggled as the irons went on.

543
00:36:28,794 --> 00:36:32,967
King Krool rode out to the hunt with
cannons, laser guns, and silver trumpets.

544
00:36:33,267 --> 00:36:35,176
From the other side came the wagon.

545
00:36:35,216 --> 00:36:38,221
Its door swung open on the
black mouth of a field gun.

546
00:36:39,821 --> 00:36:41,749
Something shot out like a sandstorm.

547
00:36:41,789 --> 00:36:44,903
The curtain floated down, its
bells ringing in the quiet.

548
00:36:46,503 --> 00:36:49,227
The beast looked like a
long, low hill of dry weeds.

549
00:36:49,527 --> 00:36:53,806
The hounds rushed at it. It opened its
eyes, and half the pack fell back smoking.

550
00:36:54,306 --> 00:36:57,075
The King rode up and cut off
its head. It grew another.

551
00:36:57,575 --> 00:36:58,377
He cut it in two.

552
00:36:58,417 --> 00:37:00,508
Now there were two
beasts, and the old head

553
00:37:00,548 --> 00:37:02,628
ran about between them on new little legs.

554
00:37:03,128 --> 00:37:04,359
He chopped it into pieces.

555
00:37:04,399 --> 00:37:08,456
The pieces backed away, huddled together,
and the beast stood there again, yawning.

556
00:37:08,956 --> 00:37:10,537
He fired his antimatter cannon.

557
00:37:10,577 --> 00:37:13,817
The beast merely sank down a
bit, and thumbed its nose at him.

558
00:37:14,417 --> 00:37:17,174
Then it leapt, and in the air
it turned into three policemen.

559
00:37:17,674 --> 00:37:20,020
THE POLICEMAN: You are
under arrest, Your Majesty.

560
00:37:20,720 --> 00:37:22,126
No one may resist an arrest.

561
00:37:22,166 --> 00:37:26,194
They packed the King into a green gig,
and it whirled away in a cloud of dust.

562
00:37:28,894 --> 00:37:32,201
By torchlight the constructors were
brought before the Great Chancellor.

563
00:37:32,501 --> 00:37:35,531
THE GREAT CHANCELLOR: For
plotting against our beloved King,

564
00:37:35,571 --> 00:37:38,282
you shall suffer every
punishment in the law at once.

565
00:37:38,682 --> 00:37:41,409
TRURL: Could you wait a little?
We are expecting a letter.

566
00:37:41,909 --> 00:37:45,245
In walked the Postmaster General
himself, every medal ringing.

567
00:37:45,545 --> 00:37:48,876
THE POSTMASTER: I am only a mannequin.
But I come from His Majesty.

568
00:37:49,276 --> 00:37:53,004
He handed over a letter, and fell into
dust. It carried the King’s own seal.

569
00:37:53,044 --> 00:37:56,366
The King was a prisoner, and the
constructors would name the terms.

570
00:37:57,166 --> 00:37:59,108
TRURL: Our chains, if you please.

571
00:38:01,408 --> 00:38:04,966
After a bath and fireworks, they read
out their terms from a little notebook.

572
00:38:05,266 --> 00:38:07,657
A ship home, loaded
with diamonds and gold.

573
00:38:07,697 --> 00:38:10,427
A band, a children’s choir,
and a cheering crowd.

574
00:38:10,467 --> 00:38:13,350
And Protozor, who would
travel with them in a cage.

575
00:38:13,750 --> 00:38:16,438
KLAPAUCIUS: Until the ship
is ready, there is no King.

576
00:38:17,138 --> 00:38:21,282
There had been nowhere safe to put the
beast’s brain, so they made all of it brain.

577
00:38:21,322 --> 00:38:25,052
And inside it they wrote a police
force, because nobody resists an arrest.

578
00:38:25,752 --> 00:38:28,466
At the spaceport the band
played and the choir sang.

579
00:38:28,506 --> 00:38:31,789
Then Klapaucius took a tooth out
of his mouth. It was a radio.

580
00:38:32,289 --> 00:38:35,570
A sandstorm came over the horizon
and stopped in front of the crowd.

581
00:38:35,610 --> 00:38:36,684
There stood the beast.

582
00:38:37,084 --> 00:38:38,904
KLAPAUCIUS: The King, if you please.

583
00:38:39,604 --> 00:38:41,030
THE BEAST: No, I don’t think so.

584
00:38:41,430 --> 00:38:43,523
It is my turn to make demands.

585
00:38:43,823 --> 00:38:46,612
KLAPAUCIUS: You have to
obey! It is in your program!

586
00:38:46,912 --> 00:38:50,150
THE BEAST: Program, shmogram. I am
heuristic and autocratic, friend.

587
00:38:50,190 --> 00:38:53,034
Talk back, and you can ride in
the green gig with the King.

588
00:38:54,334 --> 00:38:56,062
TRURL: And what exactly do you want?

589
00:38:56,262 --> 00:38:59,290
Behind Klapaucius’s back, Trurl
took out a tooth of his own.

590
00:38:59,490 --> 00:39:01,670
THE BEAST: Well, to begin
with, I would like to marry...

591
00:39:01,870 --> 00:39:05,945
TRURL: Byte and bracket, loop and
line, one, two, three, and out of time!

592
00:39:06,745 --> 00:39:09,418
The beast blinked, and tried
to hold itself together.

593
00:39:09,458 --> 00:39:13,000
A hot wind, a smell of ozone, and
there was only a little heap of ash,

594
00:39:13,040 --> 00:39:16,110
with the King in the centre of
it, dirty and close to tears.

595
00:39:17,010 --> 00:39:19,259
TRURL: That should take
you down a size or two.

596
00:39:20,359 --> 00:39:23,718
Now. Would someone kindly show
Lord Protozor to his cage?

597
00:39:36,058 --> 00:39:40,682
Trurl and Klapaucius proved that dragons
are not impossible, only improbable.

598
00:39:40,722 --> 00:39:43,467
For one to appear by
itself, you would have to

599
00:39:43,507 --> 00:39:46,414
wait sixteen quintoquadrillion
heptillion years.

600
00:39:47,414 --> 00:39:51,431
So Trurl built a probability
amplifier, and tried it in his cellar.

601
00:39:52,431 --> 00:39:54,085
TRURL: Raising the probability.

602
00:40:01,485 --> 00:40:02,359
Klapaucius!

603
00:40:02,659 --> 00:40:03,779
KLAPAUCIUS: Lowering it.

604
00:40:06,179 --> 00:40:10,993
A dragon is distinguished not by its
actuality but by its probability.

605
00:40:11,033 --> 00:40:14,430
Once it exists, that
probability is overwhelming.

606
00:40:22,930 --> 00:40:26,185
Other scholars repeated the
experiment, with less care.

607
00:40:26,225 --> 00:40:28,261
Dragons got loose, and multiplied.

608
00:40:31,261 --> 00:40:33,530
When hunters closed a ring around one,

609
00:40:33,570 --> 00:40:36,932
it slipped out of real space
into configurational space.

610
00:40:36,972 --> 00:40:39,705
All they found was a
burnt patch and a smell.

611
00:40:42,505 --> 00:40:44,605
TRURL: Have you heard
about this Basiliscus?

612
00:40:45,105 --> 00:40:46,374
KLAPAUCIUS: I don’t like it.

613
00:40:46,974 --> 00:40:51,891
TRURL: Wherever he lands, dragons appear.
Then he charges the king to remove them.

614
00:40:52,491 --> 00:40:57,312
KLAPAUCIUS: He brings them with him, at
a probability near zero, and turns it up.

615
00:40:57,912 --> 00:41:01,248
TRURL: And if he only turns
it down again, they come back.

616
00:41:01,288 --> 00:41:03,267
By then he is gone, with the gold.

617
00:41:05,867 --> 00:41:08,573
They split up. Klapaucius
did not chase dragons.

618
00:41:08,613 --> 00:41:12,845
He set dragon dampers in every square,
and soon the beasts were very rare.

619
00:41:16,245 --> 00:41:20,219
On the planet of King Pfftius he
heard that Trurl had been there first.

620
00:41:20,259 --> 00:41:24,838
Trurl had killed the dragon, the king
said, and brought twenty-four of its teeth.

621
00:41:25,438 --> 00:41:29,203
But there was some misunderstanding
about the fee. Trurl stormed out.

622
00:41:29,243 --> 00:41:32,221
That same day the dragon
came back, worse than before.

623
00:41:37,021 --> 00:41:39,345
KLAPAUCIUS: What are you
carrying, good people?

624
00:41:39,845 --> 00:41:44,206
A PORTER: The tribute for the dragon,
sir. Gold, jewels, imported perfume.

625
00:41:44,706 --> 00:41:48,674
KLAPAUCIUS: Perfume? Dragons have
no use for perfume. Or for money.

626
00:41:49,174 --> 00:41:51,239
A PORTER: It used to ask for virgins.

627
00:41:51,279 --> 00:41:53,343
That stopped after the stranger came,

628
00:41:53,383 --> 00:41:56,226
with his boxes, and a
little dial just like yours.

629
00:41:56,626 --> 00:41:59,008
MASTER GYLES: A fortnight
past, or maybe two.

630
00:42:00,208 --> 00:42:03,639
The counter on his chest settled
at eight tenths of a dragon.

631
00:42:07,239 --> 00:42:12,146
KLAPAUCIUS: A second head in its body.
Two heads always quarrel. It will freeze.

632
00:42:18,746 --> 00:42:21,254
Then I will make you improbable.

633
00:42:32,254 --> 00:42:33,799
Nothing touches it.

634
00:42:35,599 --> 00:42:36,234
TRURL: Yipe!

635
00:42:37,034 --> 00:42:38,826
Stop! Stop! Don’t kill me!

636
00:42:41,826 --> 00:42:44,115
KLAPAUCIUS: Trurl? What
is this masquerade?

637
00:42:44,715 --> 00:42:48,109
TRURL: I killed the real
dragon. The king would not pay.

638
00:42:48,149 --> 00:42:52,012
He wanted a death certificate,
an autopsy, forms in triplicate.

639
00:42:52,512 --> 00:42:57,071
So I kept its skin. A telephone
pole, some pulleys, and I was ready.

640
00:42:57,671 --> 00:43:00,589
KLAPAUCIUS: But if they would
not pay you in the first place…

641
00:43:01,589 --> 00:43:03,518
TRURL: This way I get the tribute.

642
00:43:04,718 --> 00:43:06,776
KLAPAUCIUS: It was not
right to force them.

643
00:43:07,276 --> 00:43:08,442
TRURL: Who forced them?

644
00:43:08,482 --> 00:43:12,254
I walked around in the mountains, and
in the evenings I howled a little.

645
00:43:13,054 --> 00:43:16,745
KLAPAUCIUS: Why didn’t it vanish,
when I made everything improbable?

646
00:43:17,345 --> 00:43:22,077
TRURL: Probability-proof shields, under
the skin. In case Basiliscus came by.

647
00:43:23,277 --> 00:43:27,501
Come on. I saved the platinum for
last. Now you can help me carry it.

648
00:43:45,108 --> 00:43:47,248
One night, long after
Trurl had gone to bed,

649
00:43:47,288 --> 00:43:50,259
somebody hammered on his door
hard enough to shake it loose.

650
00:43:50,759 --> 00:43:52,449
He drew the bolts, half asleep.

651
00:43:52,489 --> 00:43:56,283
A ship shaped like an enormous
pyramid had landed on his front lawn.

652
00:43:56,683 --> 00:43:59,240
Porters hurried down its
ramp with sacks of gifts,

653
00:43:59,280 --> 00:44:03,019
and stacked them so fast that the
sacks were soon taller than the house.

654
00:44:03,419 --> 00:44:06,392
Through a gap in the sacks came
a knight of polished armor,

655
00:44:06,432 --> 00:44:09,699
with jeweled eyes, swept-back
antennas, and a stole of diamonds.

656
00:44:10,099 --> 00:44:12,420
THE GRAND SENESCHAL: Do
I have the pleasure of

657
00:44:12,460 --> 00:44:14,770
addressing Trurl, the
celebrated constructor?

658
00:44:15,070 --> 00:44:17,750
TRURL: Yes. Sorry. I was
asleep. Come in, come in.

659
00:44:18,450 --> 00:44:20,915
THE GRAND SENESCHAL:
My king sends for you.

660
00:44:20,955 --> 00:44:24,100
His son is sick with love,
and you alone can cure him.

661
00:44:26,200 --> 00:44:27,443
Trurl put on a bathrobe.

662
00:44:27,483 --> 00:44:31,225
The knight sat down across from him,
and his helmet purred like a cat.

663
00:44:31,625 --> 00:44:34,847
THE GRAND SENESCHAL: First, a
small advance on your reward.

664
00:44:35,147 --> 00:44:38,949
He drew his sword, and tapped Trurl
on the shoulder once for every title.

665
00:44:39,249 --> 00:44:43,437
THE GRAND SENESCHAL: Duke of the Lesser
Drizzle. Warden of the Eleven Spoons.

666
00:44:43,477 --> 00:44:46,825
Baron Upside, and Baron Downside.
Keeper of the Royal Hiccup.

667
00:44:47,225 --> 00:44:50,893
Outside, the sacks had climbed past
the window, and the room went dim.

668
00:44:51,693 --> 00:44:54,296
TRURL: Matters of the
heart are not my field.

669
00:44:55,096 --> 00:44:57,545
THE GRAND SENESCHAL: The
prince loves Amarandina,

670
00:44:57,585 --> 00:44:59,728
the only daughter of
the Emperor next door.

671
00:44:59,768 --> 00:45:03,791
Our two kingdoms have hated each other
for centuries, so her father will refuse.

672
00:45:03,831 --> 00:45:06,420
A year has gone by, and
the prince is wasting away.

673
00:45:06,820 --> 00:45:09,323
Trurl looked out through
the gap in the sacks.

674
00:45:09,363 --> 00:45:12,238
Rows of soldiers stood on
his lawn, perfectly still.

675
00:45:12,638 --> 00:45:15,187
TRURL: Well. If the king
wishes it, then naturally.

676
00:45:15,587 --> 00:45:17,750
The knight clapped his hands with a clang.

677
00:45:17,790 --> 00:45:21,513
Armored retainers carried Trurl
aboard, and the ship rose into the sky.

678
00:45:23,913 --> 00:45:27,078
In the royal gardens stood
the Temple of Contemplation.

679
00:45:27,118 --> 00:45:30,574
In three weeks Trurl filled it
with cables, glowing screens,

680
00:45:30,614 --> 00:45:33,876
rose petals and perfume, and
named it the femfatalatron.

681
00:45:34,276 --> 00:45:38,075
Whoever stepped inside would feel
every charm in the universe at once.

682
00:45:38,115 --> 00:45:40,472
On the front, behind
glass, were the dials.

683
00:45:40,872 --> 00:45:44,787
TRURL: Power, forty megamors.
Efficiency, ninety-six percent.

684
00:45:44,827 --> 00:45:46,707
And this is the rapturometer.

685
00:45:47,107 --> 00:45:49,854
There was also an ardor
damper, a sigh condenser,

686
00:45:49,894 --> 00:45:52,510
and a swoon regulator,
each with its own knob.

687
00:45:53,010 --> 00:45:57,001
Forty elderly peers took four hours to
push the prince along the garden path.

688
00:45:57,041 --> 00:46:00,967
He kicked and butted them all the way,
so they pushed with feather pillows.

689
00:46:01,767 --> 00:46:03,773
TRURL: Every dial, all the way up.

690
00:46:05,873 --> 00:46:08,083
THE MACHINE: Five. Four.
Three. Two. One. Begin.

691
00:46:08,883 --> 00:46:09,523
The hum rose.

692
00:46:09,563 --> 00:46:12,397
Every needle climbed until
it pressed against its stop,

693
00:46:12,437 --> 00:46:13,952
and stayed there for an hour.

694
00:46:14,452 --> 00:46:15,773
Trurl watched and waited.

695
00:46:15,813 --> 00:46:17,895
He only made sure nothing went too far,

696
00:46:17,935 --> 00:46:20,819
so the prince would not fall
for the machine instead.

697
00:46:21,419 --> 00:46:22,967
At last the trapdoor opened.

698
00:46:23,007 --> 00:46:26,028
Out of a cloud of perfume,
across crushed rose petals,

699
00:46:26,068 --> 00:46:27,938
the prince stumbled, and fainted.

700
00:46:29,038 --> 00:46:30,749
PRINCE PANTAGOON: Amarandina.

701
00:46:31,949 --> 00:46:34,398
Trurl pressed the
rapturometer to his forehead.

702
00:46:34,438 --> 00:46:37,363
It read one hundred and seven,
and then the glass burst,

703
00:46:37,403 --> 00:46:39,573
and the mercury ran out, still trembling.

704
00:46:42,273 --> 00:46:44,930
That night some stonemasons
crept inside to see

705
00:46:44,970 --> 00:46:47,570
how it worked, and
switched it on by accident.

706
00:46:47,610 --> 00:46:51,167
They came out smoking, and
somebody sent for the fire brigade.

707
00:46:51,767 --> 00:46:56,097
Next Trurl built a heavier machine, all
iron, with bigger parts and bigger dials.

708
00:46:56,697 --> 00:46:58,908
THE MACHINE: Five. Four.
Three. Two. One. Begin.

709
00:46:59,408 --> 00:47:02,775
The needles climbed faster this
time, and hit their stops harder.

710
00:47:03,475 --> 00:47:05,018
PRINCE PANTAGOON: Amarandina!

711
00:47:05,518 --> 00:47:08,681
Trurl paced his room for miles,
read every manual he owned,

712
00:47:08,721 --> 00:47:10,404
and threw each one at the wall.

713
00:47:11,304 --> 00:47:15,951
TRURL: Every dial measured the prince.
The trouble was never inside the prince.

714
00:47:18,451 --> 00:47:20,350
In the morning he asked to see the king.

715
00:47:20,750 --> 00:47:24,328
TRURL: Your Majesty, I used the
strongest methods there are.

716
00:47:24,368 --> 00:47:26,861
I could build him an imitation Amarandina,

717
00:47:26,901 --> 00:47:30,096
but sooner or later he would
hear about the real one.

718
00:47:30,896 --> 00:47:35,341
He will not stop loving her as long
as he lives. So he has to marry her.

719
00:47:36,141 --> 00:47:38,780
KING PROTUBERON: Impossible.
The Emperor will never agree.

720
00:47:39,080 --> 00:47:42,749
TRURL: Not even if he had to? If he
were beaten, and begged for peace?

721
00:47:43,049 --> 00:47:46,464
KING PROTUBERON: A war, for my son’s
wedding? Two great kingdoms in blood?

722
00:47:46,504 --> 00:47:47,437
Out of the question.

723
00:47:47,837 --> 00:47:51,642
TRURL: That is the answer I hoped
for. But not every war is the same.

724
00:47:52,042 --> 00:47:56,159
Trurl leaned in and whispered his plan,
and slowly the king’s face brightened.

725
00:47:56,559 --> 00:47:59,164
KING PROTUBERON: Go and do it,
stranger. And good luck to you.

726
00:48:01,564 --> 00:48:04,978
TRURL: Not one of their people
will be killed. Quite the reverse.

727
00:48:05,778 --> 00:48:10,027
The royal foundries began casting huge
cannons, and nobody knew what for.

728
00:48:10,067 --> 00:48:13,532
They were set all around the
planet, disguised as defenses.

729
00:48:14,032 --> 00:48:16,802
Trurl lived in the royal
laboratory, day and night,

730
00:48:16,842 --> 00:48:19,046
over cauldrons that bubbled and gurgled.

731
00:48:19,546 --> 00:48:22,701
A spy at the door would have
heard only small cooing sounds,

732
00:48:22,741 --> 00:48:25,775
and seen assistants running
past with armfuls of diapers.

733
00:48:28,375 --> 00:48:31,792
Seven days later, at midnight,
the cannons lifted their muzzles

734
00:48:31,832 --> 00:48:35,234
toward the pale white star where
the Emperor ruled, and fired.

735
00:48:35,734 --> 00:48:36,928
They fired no shells.

736
00:48:36,968 --> 00:48:41,196
They fired babies, wrapped up soft,
each one on its own small parachute.

737
00:48:41,796 --> 00:48:45,024
The babies landed gently, by the
thousand, and crawled everywhere,

738
00:48:45,064 --> 00:48:47,292
and grew, and there were
always more of them.

739
00:48:47,792 --> 00:48:50,750
The nurseries filled, then the
streets, then the treasury.

740
00:48:50,790 --> 00:48:52,651
The Emperor’s economy began to sink.

741
00:48:53,151 --> 00:48:56,842
And still the bundles came down,
plump and cheerful, diapers flapping.

742
00:48:57,842 --> 00:49:00,726
THE EMPEROR: Enough. Tell him
the prince may marry my daughter.

743
00:49:03,526 --> 00:49:05,486
The cannons were plugged and stored.

744
00:49:05,526 --> 00:49:09,302
Just in case, Trurl took the
femfatalatron apart with his own hands.

745
00:49:10,202 --> 00:49:12,335
TRURL: Nobody turns this on again.

746
00:49:13,335 --> 00:49:16,676
At the wedding feast Trurl was
best man, dressed in emeralds

747
00:49:16,716 --> 00:49:20,321
from collar to cuff, and led the
toasts with a ceremonial baton.

748
00:49:20,721 --> 00:49:23,032
THE GUESTS: To the prince and Amarandina!

749
00:49:23,632 --> 00:49:25,162
PRINCE PANTAGOON: Amarandina.

750
00:49:26,062 --> 00:49:28,602
Then Trurl loaded his
rocket with all the titles

751
00:49:28,642 --> 00:49:30,953
both monarchs had given
him, and flew home.

752
00:49:49,658 --> 00:49:53,945
King Balerion ruled Cymberia, and he
was not cruel. He just liked to play.

753
00:49:53,985 --> 00:49:57,317
Cards, hopscotch, leapfrog,
and above all, hide-and-seek.

754
00:49:57,717 --> 00:50:00,160
Whenever a treaty needed
signing, the king hid,

755
00:50:00,200 --> 00:50:02,629
and the court had to
find him, or be punished.

756
00:50:02,929 --> 00:50:10,528
THE COURT: One. Two. Three.
Four. Five. Ready or not!

757
00:50:11,028 --> 00:50:14,424
Once, a war went undeclared for
three days, because the king was

758
00:50:14,464 --> 00:50:17,684
hanging in the hall in glass
drops, pretending to be a lamp.

759
00:50:18,284 --> 00:50:19,704
BALERION: Nothing here is serious.

760
00:50:19,744 --> 00:50:21,933
The crown is a counter,
and the world is the board.

761
00:50:22,833 --> 00:50:26,012
Then he took up riddles, and
gave out jobs by the answers.

762
00:50:26,412 --> 00:50:27,046
BALERION: Why is a comet
like a long speech?

763
00:50:27,086 --> 00:50:30,183
THE COURT: Because all
anyone remembers is the tail!

764
00:50:30,583 --> 00:50:33,846
Soon the whole court answered
before he had finished asking,

765
00:50:33,886 --> 00:50:35,922
and carried encyclopedias everywhere.

766
00:50:37,822 --> 00:50:42,036
He offered a prize for the finest hiding
spot anywhere: the old family diadem,

767
00:50:42,076 --> 00:50:45,347
locked in the vault so long
that the court had forgotten it.

768
00:50:45,747 --> 00:50:48,066
Trurl and Klapaucius
heard of it at an inn.

769
00:50:48,106 --> 00:50:52,328
The mob at the gate was too thick, so
the next day they came back with coins.

770
00:50:52,728 --> 00:50:54,428
TRURL: A few more coins, I think.

771
00:50:54,828 --> 00:50:58,848
KLAPAUCIUS: We should write a contract
first. With seals, and tassels.

772
00:50:59,248 --> 00:51:00,688
But the king would not wait.

773
00:51:00,728 --> 00:51:04,005
Trurl opened a box and took out
a pair of horns on a headband.

774
00:51:04,405 --> 00:51:08,421
TRURL: Wear these, touch foreheads
with someone, and you trade minds.

775
00:51:08,461 --> 00:51:11,988
One horn sends yours across,
and the other brings theirs in.

776
00:51:13,888 --> 00:51:17,034
Trurl put the horns on his own
head, to show how they worked.

777
00:51:17,434 --> 00:51:19,799
TRURL: The current runs both ways at once,

778
00:51:19,839 --> 00:51:22,025
so that each mind is emptied
at the very moment that

779
00:51:22,065 --> 00:51:22,753
BALERION: Let me try!

780
00:51:22,915 --> 00:51:26,286
TRURL: the other is poured in. Nothing
is lost, and nothing is counted twice.

781
00:51:26,326 --> 00:51:28,612
BALERION: Oh! I have your fingers.
Ten of them, and every one wiggles!

782
00:51:29,112 --> 00:51:32,168
Klapaucius listened for nearly
a minute before he understood.

783
00:51:32,208 --> 00:51:34,846
The voice was Trurl’s, and
the mouth was the king’s.

784
00:51:35,246 --> 00:51:36,152
BALERION: Catch me if you can!

785
00:51:36,652 --> 00:51:40,842
The king ran away wearing Trurl’s body,
and Trurl, tangled in a long robe,

786
00:51:40,882 --> 00:51:43,928
fell on his face, holding a
scepter he had never seen.

787
00:51:43,968 --> 00:51:46,711
The guards seized Klapaucius,
and threw him out.

788
00:51:49,311 --> 00:51:51,976
TRURL: Listen to me! The
minds have been swapped!

789
00:51:52,016 --> 00:51:55,272
The king is running about in
my body, and he has the horns!

790
00:51:55,772 --> 00:51:56,572
Nobody listened.

791
00:51:56,612 --> 00:51:59,825
The court decided that too many
riddles had unhinged the king,

792
00:51:59,865 --> 00:52:02,855
and locked him in his bedchamber
to wait for the doctors.

793
00:52:03,855 --> 00:52:07,204
TRURL: Look at the robe if you
like, but listen to the voice.

794
00:52:10,004 --> 00:52:13,177
KLAPAUCIUS: If he strikes
a stone with those horns,

795
00:52:13,217 --> 00:52:16,075
his mind goes into the
stone, and stays there.

796
00:52:16,115 --> 00:52:18,004
And the body falls down empty.

797
00:52:20,304 --> 00:52:23,251
Trurl’s body, with the king
inside, ran from the palace,

798
00:52:23,291 --> 00:52:25,744
tripped on the harbor
steps, and hurt its leg.

799
00:52:26,144 --> 00:52:28,418
BALERION: A stretcher! Feather
pillows! Fetch the royal doctors!

800
00:52:28,918 --> 00:52:33,390
The people laughed. One passing sailor
felt sorry for him, and bent down to help.

801
00:52:33,790 --> 00:52:36,187
THE SAILOR: Easy now,
friend. Let me get you up.

802
00:52:36,737 --> 00:52:37,425
Oh. My leg. Where am I?

803
00:52:37,465 --> 00:52:39,034
BALERION: Ha ha! A sailor!
And what good legs!

804
00:52:39,534 --> 00:52:43,556
He grabbed the horns off the head on the
ground, and danced away toward the harbor.

805
00:52:45,656 --> 00:52:49,833
On the quay, a shipowner knew the man as
one of his sailors, and went to scold him.

806
00:52:50,233 --> 00:52:52,486
BALERION: Name anyone you like,
and I will be them by supper.

807
00:52:53,286 --> 00:52:55,016
He hit the shipowner with a stick.

808
00:52:55,056 --> 00:52:58,960
Then the police arrived, and the
Commissioner himself ordered him arrested.

809
00:52:59,360 --> 00:53:00,535
THE COMMISSIONER: Take him away.

810
00:53:00,985 --> 00:53:03,959
I am the Commissioner! I am
in charge of this harbor!

811
00:53:03,999 --> 00:53:06,911
BALERION: Quite right! Take this
troublemaker to jail, and do not be gentle.

812
00:53:09,011 --> 00:53:12,068
Klapaucius followed the
trail to the police station.

813
00:53:12,108 --> 00:53:16,515
Inside stood a giant in a uniform too
small for him, and the giant winked.

814
00:53:16,915 --> 00:53:19,677
BALERION: I knew you the moment I
saw you! I saw you at the palace.

815
00:53:19,717 --> 00:53:21,641
Is this not the cleverest
hiding place of all?

816
00:53:22,041 --> 00:53:24,068
KLAPAUCIUS: Your Majesty, please.

817
00:53:24,108 --> 00:53:28,242
Suppose you land in someone who
is very ill, or hunted by the law.

818
00:53:28,742 --> 00:53:30,266
BALERION: One rule keeps me safe.

819
00:53:30,306 --> 00:53:32,865
I pull the horns off the old
head, and I never let go.

820
00:53:33,665 --> 00:53:36,077
Klapaucius threw an
inkwell in the king’s face,

821
00:53:36,117 --> 00:53:38,672
jumped out of the window,
and ran into the crowd.

822
00:53:40,772 --> 00:53:44,046
The palace was shut, and Trurl
was sedated for a whole day.

823
00:53:44,086 --> 00:53:48,017
So Klapaucius had himself arrested,
and asked to see the Commissioner.

824
00:53:48,417 --> 00:53:53,092
KLAPAUCIUS: Great Police Sir, help! Folk
call me Klapaucius, but me am another!

825
00:53:53,132 --> 00:53:55,818
Horned man go bang, and
now me in wrong head!

826
00:53:56,318 --> 00:53:58,564
Then he told a long
tale: a rich foreigner,

827
00:53:58,604 --> 00:54:01,739
a ship full of puzzles, and a
password for the ship’s mate.

828
00:54:02,139 --> 00:54:04,415
KLAPAUCIUS: The password is marigold.

829
00:54:04,915 --> 00:54:06,085
The king loved puzzles.

830
00:54:06,125 --> 00:54:09,965
He asked where the ship was, then
locked Klapaucius in, and went to look.

831
00:54:11,065 --> 00:54:14,809
KLAPAUCIUS: There is no ship.
An empty dock is the trap.

832
00:54:15,409 --> 00:54:17,090
BALERION: Liar! Where is the
ship? Where are the puzzles?

833
00:54:17,640 --> 00:54:20,563
KLAPAUCIUS: Guards! Lock him
up, and watch him closely.

834
00:54:20,663 --> 00:54:22,687
BALERION: Swindler! Cheat! Wait
until I get my hands on you!

835
00:54:24,987 --> 00:54:28,184
Klapaucius, in the Commissioner’s
body, got into the palace

836
00:54:28,224 --> 00:54:31,531
on urgent business, and found
Trurl asleep in the king’s bed.

837
00:54:31,571 --> 00:54:33,217
He touched the sleeper’s heel.

838
00:54:33,367 --> 00:54:34,709
TRURL: Hah! Ha ha! Stop!

839
00:54:35,109 --> 00:54:37,270
KLAPAUCIUS: Quiet. It is Klapaucius.

840
00:54:37,310 --> 00:54:40,549
I needed the uniform to get
in, and I have the horns.

841
00:54:41,249 --> 00:54:42,703
Trurl got up and gave orders.

842
00:54:42,743 --> 00:54:45,123
Trurl’s body and the
sailor’s were fetched from

843
00:54:45,163 --> 00:54:47,532
the hospital, and the
prisoner was brought in.

844
00:54:48,232 --> 00:54:50,725
TRURL: Everyone goes
home, except the king.

845
00:54:51,425 --> 00:54:52,876
The horns went round twice.

846
00:54:52,916 --> 00:54:56,740
The Commissioner came home to his
uniform, and Klapaucius to his robe.

847
00:54:56,780 --> 00:54:59,926
The king, now in a sailor,
got a long lecture and a cell.

848
00:55:00,426 --> 00:55:03,172
Next morning they told the
honest sailor everything.

849
00:55:03,212 --> 00:55:07,552
He seemed sensible, so they gave him the
crown, and Trurl took his own body back.

850
00:55:08,052 --> 00:55:09,403
THE SAILOR: I will do what I can.

851
00:55:11,703 --> 00:55:14,477
Then Klapaucius had a big
cuckoo clock carried in.

852
00:55:14,517 --> 00:55:16,504
The king’s mind went into its works,

853
00:55:16,544 --> 00:55:19,301
and the clock’s mind went
into the sailor’s body.

854
00:55:19,801 --> 00:55:21,019
BALERION: What have you done? Let me out!

855
00:55:21,919 --> 00:55:23,307
THE CLOCK’S MIND: Yes, sir. At once, sir.

856
00:55:23,807 --> 00:55:26,551
The sailor’s body went back
to the docks and hauled rope

857
00:55:26,591 --> 00:55:29,175
perfectly well, which says
something about the work.

858
00:55:30,175 --> 00:55:33,656
BALERION: Every hour I announce the hour,
and every hour I must announce it again.

859
00:55:34,256 --> 00:55:36,976
That was the king’s work
from then on, day and night,

860
00:55:37,016 --> 00:55:39,098
driven by the little teeth of the gears.

861
00:55:41,598 --> 00:55:45,814
The friends said goodbye to the crowned
sailor, and left that troublesome kingdom.

862
00:55:46,214 --> 00:55:49,048
KLAPAUCIUS: Trurl. The
diadem. Where did you find it?

863
00:55:49,448 --> 00:55:52,683
TRURL: In the vault. Kings can
open the vault, and I was one.

864
00:55:53,583 --> 00:55:57,158
The finest place to hide a
thing was the king himself,

865
00:55:57,198 --> 00:55:59,608
and no one ever looks inside a king.

866
00:56:00,408 --> 00:56:03,596
Far behind them, in the great
hall, a clock struck the hour.

867
00:56:22,725 --> 00:56:27,227
Beyond a green star, close to a white
sun, lived a people called the Steelypips.

868
00:56:27,267 --> 00:56:31,695
They had no quarrels and no lessons.
They lived on, in, and under one machine.

869
00:56:32,395 --> 00:56:34,810
It was a fine machine
of gears and springs.

870
00:56:34,850 --> 00:56:37,419
They had built it from
their own saved atoms.

871
00:56:38,319 --> 00:56:41,565
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: We made it
ourselves, so there is nothing in it to fix.

872
00:56:42,465 --> 00:56:44,776
One day a comet came by, in a lace bonnet,

873
00:56:44,816 --> 00:56:47,239
with four long tails
and a smell of almonds.

874
00:56:47,279 --> 00:56:50,357
She said she would burn them
to the ground, as a start.

875
00:56:50,957 --> 00:56:53,907
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: One moment,
madam. This machine has no faults.

876
00:56:53,947 --> 00:56:55,361
Please leave, or you will be sorry.

877
00:56:56,161 --> 00:57:00,144
She filled the sky with fire, until
their old moon curled up at both tips.

878
00:57:00,184 --> 00:57:04,588
So the Steelypips wrapped their strongest
fields around it, and threw the switch.

879
00:57:06,988 --> 00:57:11,262
The sky cleared with a bang. All that
was left of the comet was a bit of ash.

880
00:57:11,302 --> 00:57:13,655
The Steelypips went back to being pleased.

881
00:57:16,055 --> 00:57:20,613
Some time later, something came down
from the sky. Nobody knew what it was.

882
00:57:20,653 --> 00:57:25,373
It landed on the highest peak, very
heavily, and nobody wanted a second look.

883
00:57:26,073 --> 00:57:28,813
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: Pardon
us, but this is a very good machine.

884
00:57:28,853 --> 00:57:29,558
Please move along.

885
00:57:30,658 --> 00:57:34,007
It did not move. It did not
attack either. It just stayed.

886
00:57:34,807 --> 00:57:38,467
So, to save money, they sent a
small scarechrome to frighten it.

887
00:57:38,967 --> 00:57:40,967
THE SCARECHROME: Fear me.
I am terrible. Fear me.

888
00:57:41,767 --> 00:57:45,650
It hissed and spat and frightened
itself a little. The thing stayed.

889
00:57:45,690 --> 00:57:48,978
It tried a second frequency,
but its heart was not in it.

890
00:57:51,178 --> 00:57:53,190
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON:
Something heavier, then.

891
00:57:53,230 --> 00:57:55,913
Hydraulic, with plenty of muscle,
and a scarechrome on top for luck.

892
00:57:56,613 --> 00:58:01,000
The great engine rolled out. It wound
itself up, and counted down from four.

893
00:58:04,900 --> 00:58:07,676
Oil bubbled and gears
rattled. Not a scratch.

894
00:58:08,476 --> 00:58:10,517
The Steelypips held a council of war.

895
00:58:10,557 --> 00:58:14,285
They built a machine to build a
machine to build a machine so large

896
00:58:14,325 --> 00:58:18,150
that the nearest stars stepped back,
with a servospook at its heart.

897
00:58:18,750 --> 00:58:21,768
THE SERVOSPOOK: I am the
servospook. I mean business.

898
00:58:22,368 --> 00:58:24,637
It gathered all its strength, and let go.

899
00:58:34,037 --> 00:58:37,512
When the dark cleared, there was only
scrap metal, ticking as it cooled.

900
00:58:38,812 --> 00:58:43,249
Each weapon had been bigger than the
last. The thing was the same size.

901
00:58:45,249 --> 00:58:47,450
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON:
Then we must be patient.

902
00:58:47,490 --> 00:58:50,204
We will send a cyberivy, to creep
up quietly and take its time.

903
00:58:51,004 --> 00:58:53,969
The ivy crept up as if it
had nothing on its mind.

904
00:58:54,009 --> 00:58:57,575
It sent out a root, then another,
and closed in from behind.

905
00:58:57,615 --> 00:59:01,582
It went exactly as planned, and
when it was over, it was not over.

906
00:59:02,282 --> 00:59:04,852
They made nets and
glues, lassos and screws,

907
00:59:04,892 --> 00:59:07,936
traps and walls, and turned
the machine upside down.

908
00:59:07,976 --> 00:59:08,866
Nothing helped.

909
00:59:10,366 --> 00:59:12,929
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: This
has never happened to us before.

910
00:59:12,969 --> 00:59:14,611
We do not know what to do with our hands.

911
00:59:17,011 --> 00:59:20,242
Then someone came along on
a vehicle nobody could name.

912
00:59:20,282 --> 00:59:23,870
It had a horse’s head, one
bicycle wheel and a rocket’s tail.

913
00:59:23,910 --> 00:59:26,587
The rider was Trurl,
out on one of his trips.

914
00:59:27,187 --> 00:59:31,052
THE STEELYPIPS: We tried
everything. It is still there.

915
00:59:31,752 --> 00:59:34,312
TRURL: Did you try anything
that was not a weapon?

916
00:59:34,812 --> 00:59:37,599
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: What else is
there? We have gears, springs, and force.

917
00:59:38,299 --> 00:59:42,153
TRURL: I can stay a little while,
as an adviser. Is that agreeable?

918
00:59:42,653 --> 00:59:45,941
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: Of course!
Shall we bring hammers, artillery, dynamite?

919
00:59:45,981 --> 00:59:47,825
Coffee or tea? The vending
machine makes both.

920
00:59:48,425 --> 00:59:51,335
TRURL: Coffee. Not for me,
for the job. The rest, no.

921
00:59:52,335 --> 00:59:55,014
Force has failed, so we will use forms.

922
00:59:55,814 --> 00:59:59,446
I need paper and ink, stamps
and seals, a teller’s window,

923
00:59:59,486 --> 01:00:02,358
a zinc spoon, a saucer,
coffee, and a mailman.

924
01:00:02,398 --> 01:00:04,171
And something to write with.

925
01:00:04,571 --> 01:00:06,061
THE STEELYPIPS: We will fetch it!

926
01:00:08,261 --> 01:00:11,109
Trurl sat down, and
dictated the first notice.

927
01:00:11,709 --> 01:00:16,172
TRURL: Regarding the obstruction
of these premises, under Form Four,

928
01:00:16,212 --> 01:00:21,005
subsection Q: all services to the
occupant are withdrawn, pending review.

929
01:00:21,045 --> 01:00:24,290
Appeals must be in writing,
on the correct forms.

930
01:00:24,990 --> 01:00:29,115
He sealed it, stamped it, entered it in
the ledger, and checked the register.

931
01:00:29,155 --> 01:00:30,724
Then he sent for the mailman.

932
01:00:31,424 --> 01:00:33,843
The mailman took it away. They waited.

933
01:00:39,443 --> 01:00:40,564
TRURL: Was it delivered?

934
01:00:40,964 --> 01:00:41,859
THE MAILMAN: It was.

935
01:00:42,259 --> 01:00:43,219
TRURL: And the receipt?

936
01:00:43,619 --> 01:00:46,083
THE MAILMAN: Signed, on this
line. And here is its appeal.

937
01:00:47,683 --> 01:00:52,311
TRURL: Once it took delivery of the first
notice, it belonged to the paperwork.

938
01:00:53,211 --> 01:00:55,705
Without reading the
appeal, he sent it back.

939
01:00:55,745 --> 01:00:58,641
Across the front he wrote,
in a hurry, wrong forms,

940
01:00:58,681 --> 01:01:01,157
and signed with a scrawl
no one could read.

941
01:01:01,757 --> 01:01:02,775
TRURL: Now, to work.

942
01:01:05,375 --> 01:01:08,250
The mailman ran all day.
Trurl stamped and filed.

943
01:01:08,290 --> 01:01:13,307
The typewriter chattered, and an office grew
out of nothing: stamps and rubber bands,

944
01:01:13,347 --> 01:01:17,630
paper clips, pigeonholes, filing
cabinets, signs, coffee rings, cobwebs.

945
01:01:18,130 --> 01:01:21,642
Notices went out, then
reminders, then final demands.

946
01:01:27,642 --> 01:01:29,911
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: It
is smaller. It really is smaller.

947
01:01:31,111 --> 01:01:34,731
TRURL: Next, please. The window
is closed. Come back in an hour.

948
01:01:36,931 --> 01:01:40,923
The coffee went cold. An official
was offered a bribe, and filed it.

949
01:01:40,963 --> 01:01:44,521
New cabinets arrived, and cobwebs
reached from wall to wall.

950
01:01:47,521 --> 01:01:51,866
The typewriter typed an order that the
occupant must leave, and may not appeal.

951
01:02:07,375 --> 01:02:10,582
Then Trurl stood up and threw
it all out into deep space:

952
01:02:10,622 --> 01:02:14,113
the desks, the chairs, the stamps,
the seals, the pigeonholes.

953
01:02:14,153 --> 01:02:15,976
Only the vending machine stayed.

954
01:02:16,476 --> 01:02:18,684
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: What are
you doing? We had grown used to it!

955
01:02:19,284 --> 01:02:21,177
TRURL: Take a look instead, friends.

956
01:02:22,177 --> 01:02:24,105
They looked, and the thing was gone.

957
01:02:24,145 --> 01:02:27,550
It had shrunk and shrunk, until
they needed a magnifying glass.

958
01:02:27,590 --> 01:02:31,582
All they could find was one small damp
spot, where something had dripped.

959
01:02:32,182 --> 01:02:34,934
THE STEELYPIP SPOKESPERSON: But why throw
out the papers, and pour out the coffee?

960
01:02:35,834 --> 01:02:38,897
TRURL: Throw it all out, or
the office will start on you.

961
01:02:39,797 --> 01:02:44,124
He nodded to them kindly, climbed onto
his odd vehicle, and flew away, smiling.

